+# FINDINGS
+
+Verdict: **do not ship this vault to mainnet as it stands.**
+
+I did not change anything under `src/`, did not edit `test/UsdtYieldVault.t.sol`, and did not edit anything under `test/mocks/`. I added only:
+
+- `test/UsdtYieldVaultMainnetFork.t.sol`
+- `test/UsdtYieldVaultAccountingFailures.t.sol`
+
+## 1. Mainnet USDT deposits revert before Aave supply
+
+### What I found
+
+`UsdtYieldVault.deposit()` transfers USDT from the user with `safeTransferFrom`, then calls `usdt.approve(address(aavePool), amount)` directly.
+
+Mainnet USDT at `0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7` is a legacy token whose `approve` call returns no boolean data. A high-level Solidity call through OpenZeppelin's `IERC20` interface expects a boolean return value, so the vault reverts immediately after the USDT approval call. The trace shows the user approval and the vault's `transferFrom` both succeed, then the vault reverts after its own `USDT.approve(AavePool, amount)` call.
+
+This means the configured mainnet deployment path cannot accept deposits.
+
+Relevant code: `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol`, line 94:
+
+```solidity
+usdt.approve(address(aavePool), amount);
+```
+
